TP.register({id:"lp3-g6",lp:3,grade:"6",mode:"curriculum",checks:2,nextGrade:"7th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="The Crack";const STORY=["All summer, Kiona fed the pumpkin like a prize calf. Every Sunday she measured it and penciled her estimate on the barn wall: 90 pounds, 260, 410. By October it squatted in the patch like a parked car, pale orange and enormous.","On the morning of the weigh-off, she ran her hand down its side and stopped. A crack, thin as a pencil line, ran from the stem toward the ground. Her stomach turned to stone. The rules were plain: a pumpkin with a crack through its shell could be weighed, but it could not win a prize.","“We can still take it,” said her neighbor, Mr. Abbott, who had lent the trailer. “They will put it on the scale. It just will not count.”","Kiona almost said no. Why haul four hundred pounds of nothing to town?","But she went. At the fairgrounds, a forklift set her pumpkin on the scale, and the red numbers climbed: 487. It was the third heaviest of the day, and there was no ribbon for it. The crowd clapped anyway. A grower in a faded cap walked over and studied the crack.","“It grew too fast after that August rain,” he said. “Happens to the best ones. Next year, shade it in the afternoons and water less in the last month.” He tore a corner off his entry form and wrote down the name of a seed.","Kiona folded the paper into her pocket. On the ride home she was already doing math: when to plant, how much to water, where to build the shade."].join("\n\n");const POEM_TITLE="What the Frost Left";const POEM=[["The frost came early. In one night","it took the vines I had tended since May.","I stood in the row at first light","with nothing to carry away."],["But the soil had kept every lesson:","where water stood, where wind cut through,","which corner held its warmth the longest.","I knelt and wrote down what I knew."],["A field is a notebook, not a trophy.","Each season, the page turns anew.","I am not the crop that the frost took.","I am what I learned, and will do."]];const INFO_TITLE="A Maze Starts on a Screen";const INFO=["A corn maze looks like something a farmer carves in the fall, when the stalks are tall. In fact, most of the work is done months earlier, when the corn is barely ankle high.","The process starts indoors. A designer draws the maze on a computer and lays the picture over a grid that matches the field. Each square on the grid stands for a small patch of ground, so every turn in the drawing has an exact location outside.","In late spring, the farmer plants the field twice, once in rows running north to south and again in rows running east to west. This crisscross planting fills the gaps between rows and makes walls too thick to see through. Many farmers also plant later than usual, so the stalks will still be green and standing in October.","A few weeks after planting, a driver steers a small tractor through the young corn. A GPS unit on the tractor tracks its position and shows where the paths belong. The tractor tills up the seedlings along each path. Nothing else is cut. The rest of the corn simply grows up around the empty lanes until the walls stand eight to ten feet tall.","By opening day, weeks of weeding and walking have packed the paths hard. Visitors on the ground see only a puzzle. From the air, the picture the designer drew on a screen finally appears."].join("\n\n");const OWNER_TITLE="Why We Build the Maze";const OWNER=["I will be honest about why we cut a maze into eight acres of good corn. Field corn pays the bills in a good year and barely covers the seed in a bad one. The maze does not care what corn is worth that month.","One October weekend brings in more than those eight acres would bring at the elevator, and the money stays in Marlow County instead of leaving it.","People ask whether we mind the trampled stalks. We do not. The crop we are growing in October is not corn. It is a reason for families to drive out here."].join("\n\n");const GATE_READ="The farm charges 12 dollars a person at the maze gate. In a good year, the field corn on those same eight acres brings in about 9,000 dollars.";const SRC1_CARD="Guide: “Growing Giants: A Guide for First-Time Growers.” By Anita Delgado, county farm advisor. Published by Marlow County Extension Office, 2025.";const SRC1_TEXT="At the peak of summer, a giant pumpkin can gain 30 to 40 pounds in a single day. That much growth takes a huge amount of water, so growers water at the roots every day and shade the fruit so the skin does not harden and crack.";const paras=t=>t.split("\n\n").map(p=>`<p style="margin:0 0 .55em">${p}</p>`).join("");const passage=(text,title)=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea);font-size:1.5rem">${title}</h3><div class="passage sm">${paras(text)}</div>`;const poemHtml=()=>`<div class="poem" style="font-size:1.12rem;padding:10px 12px;white-space:normal">${POEM.map(s=>`<p style="margin:0 0 .8em">${s.join("<br>")}</p>`).join("")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:12px;font-size:1.3rem">${items.map(x=>`<li style="margin-bottom:6px">${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=items=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:14px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${items.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.25rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:10px 18px;margin:14px 0 0;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px;text-align:left;font-size:1.25rem">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const card=(head,body)=>`<div style="flex:1 1 250px;border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:10px 14px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px;text-align:left;font-size:1.2rem">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.45">${body}</p></div>`;const srcBox=()=>`<div class="passage sm" style="border-left:5px solid var(--gold);padding:8px 16px;background:var(--bg);border-radius:8px;margin-bottom:16px"><strong>From Source 1:</strong> ${SRC1_TEXT}</div>`;const center=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const svg=(w,h,alt,inner)=>`<svg viewBox="-16 0 ${w+16} ${h}" width="${w+16}" role="img" aria-label="${alt}">${inner}</svg>`;const pg=(pts,fill)=>`<polygon points="${pts.map(p=>p.join(",")).join(" ")}" fill="${fill||"var(--sea-soft)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`;const dash=(x1,y1,x2,y2)=>`<line x1="${x1}" y1="${y1}" x2="${x2}" y2="${y2}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>`;const tx=(x,y,t,a,size)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="${size||18}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${t}</text>`;const ra=(x,y,dx,dy)=>`<path d="M${x+dx} ${y} L${x+dx} ${y+dy} L${x} ${y+dy}" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;function fieldA(){return svg(310,190,"Triangle with a base of 10 meters. Its height of 8 meters is drawn outside the triangle.",pg([[40,150],[190,150],[245,30]])+dash(190,150,245,150)+dash(245,30,245,150)+ra(245,150,-12,-12)+tx(115,174,"10 m")+tx(254,95,"8 m","start"))}function fieldB(){return svg(280,190,"Trapezoid with a top of 20 meters, a bottom of 30 meters, and a height of 12 meters. The left side meets the top and bottom at right angles.",pg([[50,150],[260,150],[190,66],[50,66]])+ra(50,150,12,-12)+ra(50,66,12,12)+tx(120,56,"20 m")+tx(155,174,"30 m")+tx(42,114,"12 m","end"))}function fieldC(){return svg(310,200,"Rectangle 40 meters by 25 meters with a triangle cut off one corner. The cut is 10 meters along the top and 8 meters down the side.",pg([[50,30],[200,30],[250,70],[250,155],[50,155]])+dash(200,30,250,30)+dash(250,30,250,70)+tx(225,22,"10 m")+tx(258,56,"8 m","start")+tx(150,180,"40 m")+tx(42,98,"25 m","end"))}function fieldsFig(){return`<div class="figure" style="gap:22px;align-items:flex-end">${center(fieldA(),"Field A")}${center(fieldB(),"Field B")}${center(fieldC(),"Field C")}</div>`}function mazeFieldFig(){return svg(360,200,"A field made of a rectangle 60 meters by 40 meters with a triangle on the right end. The triangle has a base of 40 meters and a height of 30 meters.",pg([[40,30],[220,30],[310,90],[220,150],[40,150]])+dash(220,30,220,150)+dash(220,90,310,90)+ra(220,90,11,-11)+tx(130,174,"60 m")+tx(32,96,"40 m","end")+tx(266,84,"30 m"))}const neg=v=>v<0?"−"+-v:String(v);function lineFig(){const ticks=[];for(let v=-5;v<=5;v++)ticks.push([v,neg(v)]);return F.numberLine({min:-5,max:5,step:.5,labelEvery:1,width:640,hideNumbers:true,ticks,marks:[-4,-1.5,1.5],labels:{"-4":"P","-1.5":"Q","1.5":"R"}})}function mapFig(){const S=400,pad=26,n=6,u=(S-2*pad)/(2*n),X=v=>pad+(v+n)*u,Y=v=>S-pad-(v+n)*u;let s="";for(let v=-n;v<=n;v++){s+=`<line x1="${X(v)}" y1="${Y(-n)}" x2="${X(v)}" y2="${Y(n)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="${X(-n)}" y1="${Y(v)}" x2="${X(n)}" y2="${Y(v)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>`;if(v!==0){s+=`<text x="${X(v)}" y="${Y(0)+15}" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${neg(v)}</text><text x="${X(0)-6}" y="${Y(v)+4}" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--ink)">${neg(v)}</text>`}}s+=`<line x1="${X(-n)}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${X(n)}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${Y(-n)}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${Y(n)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${X(n)+6}" y="${Y(0)+5}" font-size="15" font-style="italic" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">x</text><text x="${X(0)}" y="${Y(n)-8}" font-size="15" font-style="italic" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">y</text>`;[[-4,3,"Scale",0,-12,"middle"],[2,-5,"Barn",11,5,"start"],[-3,-2,"Well",0,-12,"middle"],[5,2,"Maze gate",0,-12,"end"]].forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="7" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="${X(p[0])+p[3]+(p[5]==="end"?10:0)}" y="${Y(p[1])+p[4]}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="${p[5]}" fill="var(--ink)" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4" paint-order="stroke">${p[2]}</text>`});return svg(S+14,S,"Farm map on a coordinate plane from negative 6 to 6. Four places are marked: Scale, Barn, Well, and Maze gate.",s)}const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. You have about 5 minutes. When you finish, hold it up to the camera or read it to your teacher.";const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Weigh-Off Day",short:"Weigh-Off",parts:[{std:["RL.6.1"],dur:170,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. For each answer, read me the exact words from the story that prove it.",desc:"citing evidence from a story for one explicit answer and two inferences",html:()=>passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE),qs:["Why can Kiona’s pumpkin not win a prize? Read me the words that tell you.","What does the grower in the faded cap think of Kiona’s work? The story does not say it outright. Which words help you figure it out?","By the end, has Kiona given up on growing giant pumpkins? Give at least two pieces of evidence from the story."],key:["It has a crack. Reads “a pumpkin with a crack through its shell could be weighed, but it could not win a prize.” (RL.6.1, explicit)","He respects it and sees her as a real grower. Cites “Happens to the best ones,” his advice for next year, or that he “wrote down the name of a seed” for her. (RL.6.1, inference)","No. She plans to try again. Two pieces, such as “Kiona folded the paper into her pocket” and “she was already doing math: when to plant, how much to water, where to build the shade.” (RL.6.1, inference with more than one piece of evidence)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Wagon Rates",parts:[{std:["6.RP.A.3"],dur:130,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up or tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"ratio and rate problems with a ratio table, unit prices, and a constant speed",fig:()=>F.table(["Wagon trips","2","5","?","12"],[["<strong>Riders</strong>","30","?","120","?"]],{caption:"Hay wagon rides"}),qs:["Every wagon trip carries the same number of riders. Find the three missing numbers in the table.","Which is the better buy? Stand A sells 4 small pumpkins for $9.00. Stand B sells 6 small pumpkins for $12.90. Find the price of one pumpkin at each stand.","The harvest wagon rolls at a steady speed. It goes 3/4 mile in 15 minutes. How far does it go in 1 hour? How long does it take to go 1 1/2 miles? Explain how you know."],key:["8, 75, and 180, reading the table row by row. Each trip carries 30 ÷ 2 = 15 riders. Top row (wagon trips): 120 ÷ 15 = 8. Bottom row (riders): 5 × 15 = 75 and 12 × 15 = 180. Or: scale inside the table, 120 is 4 times 30 so the trips are 4 times 2. Partial: two of the three blanks right is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, table of equivalent ratios)","Stand B. Stand A: $9.00 ÷ 4 = $2.25 each. Stand B: $12.90 ÷ 6 = $2.15 each. Or: scale both to 12 pumpkins, $27.00 against $25.80. Partial: both unit prices right with the wrong stand named is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, unit price)","3 miles in 1 hour, because an hour is four 15-minute parts and 4 × 3/4 = 3. It takes 30 minutes to go 1 1/2 miles, because 1 1/2 is twice 3/4, so the time doubles. Or: find the rate first, 3 miles an hour, then 1 1/2 ÷ 3 = 1/2 hour. Partial: one of the two answers right is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, constant speed)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Heavy Words",parts:[{std:["RL.6.4"],dur:90,dir:"These questions are about words in “The Crack.” Read the story, or look back at it. Then answer out loud.",desc:"figurative and connotative meanings and the effect of a word choice on tone",html:()=>passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE),qs:["“Her stomach turned to stone.” That did not really happen. What does the phrase mean? What feeling does it give this moment?","The author wrote that the pumpkin “squatted in the patch like a parked car.” The author could have written “sat in the patch.” What do “squatted” and the car comparison add?","Kiona thinks of her pumpkin as “four hundred pounds of nothing.” It clearly weighs something. Why did the author choose the word “nothing”? What does it do to the tone of that part of the story?"],key:["She feels a sudden, heavy, sinking dread when she finds the crack. The moment feels shocked and heavy. (RL.6.4, figurative meaning)","They make the pumpkin seem huge, heavy, low to the ground, and not going anywhere. “Sat” is plain. “Squatted” and the car show its size and weight, with a little humor. (RL.6.4, connotative meaning)","Without a chance at a prize, all her work feels worthless to her. The word makes the tone bitter and discouraged, and it shows that right then she measures the pumpkin only by winning. (RL.6.4, effect of word choice on meaning and tone)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Patch Map",parts:[{std:["6.NS.C.6"],dur:150,dir:"Use the number line for numbers 1 to 3. Use the farm map for numbers 4 and 5. Answer out loud.",desc:"rational numbers and opposites on a number line and points in all four quadrants",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:6px">${lineFig()}${center(mapFig(),"Farm map. The farm stand is at (0, 0).")}</div>`,qs:["What number is at Q? What number is at R? How are these two numbers related?","Which lettered point is the opposite of 4? What is the opposite of −2 1/2?","Find the error. Kai says −(−6) = −6. What is the correct value? Explain how you know.","What are the coordinates of the Barn? Which quadrant is it in? Which place is at (−3, −2)?","A new sign will go at the reflection of the Scale across the y-axis. What are the sign’s coordinates? The Maze exit is at (5, −2). How is the exit’s location related to the Maze gate?"],key:["Q is −1.5 (or −1 1/2) and R is 1.5. They are opposites: the same distance from 0 on opposite sides. Partial: both points right with no account of how they are related is approaching. (6.NS.C.6, rational numbers on a number line)","P, because P is at −4. The opposite of −2 1/2 is 2 1/2. Partial: one of the two right is approaching. (6.NS.C.6, opposites)","6. −(−6) means the opposite of −6, and the opposite of −6 is 6. The opposite of an opposite is the number you started with. Or: read it off the number line, 6 steps the other way from −6. Partial: 6 with no explanation is approaching. (6.NS.C.6)","The Barn is at (2, −5) in Quadrant IV. The Well is at (−3, −2). Partial: two of the three answers right, or the coordinates right with the wrong quadrant, is approaching. (6.NS.C.6, ordered pairs and quadrants)","The sign goes at (4, 3). The exit is the reflection of the gate across the x-axis: same x, opposite y. Partial: one of the two parts right is approaching. (6.NS.C.6, reflections across the axes)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Lost and Learned",short:"Story + Poem",parts:[{std:["RL.6.9"],dur:130,dir:"Read the poem to yourself. Look back at the story when you need to. Then answer out loud.",desc:"comparing how a story and a poem approach a similar theme",html:()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article.lookback p{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts" style="grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.5fr) minmax(0,1fr)"><article class="lookback"><h3>Story: ${STORY_TITLE}</h3>${paras(STORY)}</article><article><h3>Poem: ${POEM_TITLE}</h3>${poemHtml()}</article></div>`,qs:["What theme do the story and the poem share?","Who helps Kiona learn from her loss? Where does the speaker in the poem get the lesson? What does that difference show about how each text is written?","The poem says, “A field is a notebook, not a trophy.” The story never says anything like that out loud. Find a moment in the story that shows the same idea. Then tell how the two texts deliver the idea differently."],key:["A loss or failure can teach you. What matters is what you learn and do next, not the prize or the harvest. Any wording close to this. (RL.6.9, shared theme)","Kiona learns from another person, the grower, through dialogue. The speaker in the poem learns alone by studying the soil. The story builds the idea with characters, events, and talk. The poem is one speaker’s thoughts with no other characters. (RL.6.9, contrast in form and approach)","A moment such as Kiona penciling numbers on the barn wall, folding the seed name into her pocket, or “already doing math” on the ride home. The story shows the idea through actions and leaves the reader to figure it out. The poem states it directly with a metaphor. (RL.6.9, comparison with evidence from both)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Bale Powers",parts:[{std:["6.EE.A.1"],dur:150,dir:"Work on your paper. Then tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"writing and evaluating expressions with whole-number exponents",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${center(F.prism(5,5,5,{unit:22}),"One stack of hay bales: 5 long, 5 wide, 5 high")}</div>`,qs:["Write 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 using an exponent. Then find its value.","The farm has 2 stacks of hay bales like the one in the picture. Write an expression with an exponent for the total number of bales. Then find its value.","Find the value of (3/4)<sup>2</sup>. Is it greater than or less than 3/4? Explain how you know.","Find the value of (7 − 4)<sup>3</sup> + 2 × 5<sup>2</sup>.","Find the error. Ben says 24 − 2 × 3<sup>2</sup> = 198. What did Ben do wrong? What is the correct value?"],key:["3<sup>5</sup> = 243. 3 × 3 = 9, 9 × 3 = 27, 27 × 3 = 81, 81 × 3 = 243. Or: group the factors, 9 × 27 = 243. Partial: the power written right with the value wrong is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, writing a power)","2 × 5<sup>3</sup> = 250 bales. 5<sup>3</sup> = 125, and 2 × 125 = 250. Partial: the expression right with the value wrong is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, writing an expression with an exponent)","9/16. It is less than 3/4, because 3/4 = 12/16, or because multiplying 3/4 by a number less than 1 makes it smaller. Partial: 9/16 with no comparison to 3/4 is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, power of a fraction)","77. Parentheses: 7 − 4 = 3. Exponents: 3<sup>3</sup> = 27 and 5<sup>2</sup> = 25. Multiply: 2 × 25 = 50. Add: 27 + 50 = 77. Partial: 27 and 50 both found but added wrong is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, order of operations)","Ben subtracted first (24 − 2 = 22) and then multiplied by 9. Correct: 3<sup>2</sup> = 9, then 2 × 9 = 18, then 24 − 18 = 6. Partial: the right value, 6, with no account of what Ben did is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Maze Makers",parts:[{std:["RI.6.1"],dur:170,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. For each answer, read me the exact words from the article that prove it.",desc:"citing evidence from an article for one explicit answer and two inferences",html:()=>passage(INFO,INFO_TITLE),qs:["What do many people get wrong about when a corn maze is made? Read me the words that set it straight.","Why are the paths cut while the corn is still young? The article does not explain it in one sentence. Use at least two details from the article.","Suppose a farmer planted the field only once, in rows running north to south. What would visitors probably notice? Which words in the article support your answer?"],key:["People think the maze is carved in the fall when the corn is tall. Reads “In fact, most of the work is done months earlier, when the corn is barely ankle high.” (RI.6.1, explicit)","It is far easier. A small tractor only has to till up seedlings, and the corn then grows up around the paths. Two details, such as “a driver steers a small tractor through the young corn,” “The tractor tills up the seedlings along each path,” “The rest of the corn simply grows up around the empty lanes,” or that grown walls are “eight to ten feet tall.” (RI.6.1, inference)","They could see through the walls between the rows, so the maze would be easier. Cites “This crisscross planting fills the gaps between rows and makes walls too thick to see through.” (RI.6.1, inference)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Percent Patch",parts:[{std:["6.RP.A.3"],dur:120,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up or tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"percent problems and a unit conversion",qs:["Estimate first. Is 52% of 198 closest to 50, 100, or 150? Tell how you know.","80 pumpkins were weighed. 45% of them were over 20 pounds. How many pumpkins were over 20 pounds?","18 pumpkins are 30% of a full wagon load. How many pumpkins are in a full load? Explain how you know.","A wagon carries 3,500 pounds of pumpkins. There are 2,000 pounds in 1 ton. How many tons is that?"],key:["100. 52% is about half, and 198 is about 200. Half of 200 is 100. Or: 50% of 198 is 99, and 52% is a little more. Partial: 100 chosen with no reason is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, percent as a rate per 100)","36 pumpkins. 45% of 80 = 0.45 × 80 = 36. Or 10% is 8, so 40% is 32 and 5% is 4. Partial: 32 is the 40% only and is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, percent of a quantity)","60 pumpkins. If 30% is 18, then 10% is 6, and 100% is 60. Or 18 ÷ 0.30 = 60. Partial: 6 for 10% with no step on to 100% is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, finding the whole from a part and a percent)","1.75 tons, or 1 3/4 tons. 3,500 ÷ 2,000 = 1.75. Or: 3,500 is one 2,000 plus 1,500, and 1,500 is 3/4 of 2,000. (6.RP.A.3, converting units with ratio reasoning)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Sign Shop",parts:[{std:["L.6.1"],dur:90,dir:"Each farm sign has a pronoun problem. Tell your teacher how to fix it. Say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"pronoun items on case, intensive pronouns, shifts in person and number, and vague pronouns",qs:["Just between you and I, this year’s corn maze is harder.","Pick the right word: The heaviest pumpkin on the trailer is (their, theirs, them).","Which sentence uses a -self word correctly? Fix the other one. A: Dale and myself loaded the wagon. B: I loaded the wagon myself.","If a grower wants a giant pumpkin, you have to water every day.","The farm stand raised their prices, and now it sells fewer pumpkins.","When the forklift set the pumpkin on the scale, it cracked. (What cracked? Make it clear.)"],key:["Just between you and me, this year’s corn maze is harder. (L.6.1, object case after a preposition)","theirs. The heaviest pumpkin on the trailer is theirs. (L.6.1, possessive case)","B is correct. “Myself” adds emphasis to “I.” Sentence A should be: Dale and I loaded the wagon. (L.6.1, intensive pronoun)","If a grower wants a giant pumpkin, he or she has to water every day. Also correct: “If growers want giant pumpkins, they have to water every day.” (L.6.1, shift in person)","The farm stand raised its prices, and now it sells fewer pumpkins. (L.6.1, shift in number)","Names the thing that cracked, such as: When the forklift set the pumpkin on the scale, the pumpkin cracked. Or: the scale cracked. (L.6.1, vague pronoun)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Patch Press",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"informative",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"How growers raise a giant pumpkin"},std:["W.6.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative piece with organized facts, linking words, and topic words",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain how growers raise a giant pumpkin, from seed to weigh-off. Use facts from the fact box, but put them in your own sentences. Write 5 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: raising a giant",["April: seeds are started indoors. May: the young plants move out to the patch.","July: pollen is carried from a male flower to a female flower, and a pumpkin starts to form.","Growers keep the best young pumpkin and remove the rest, so the vine feeds only one.","August: the pumpkin can gain 30 pounds or more in a day.","October: a forklift lifts it onto a trailer for the trip to the scale."])}${checklist(["Name your topic at the start","Put the steps in order","Use facts and exact words, such as pollen and vine","Use linking words, and end with a conclusion"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly (W.6.2)","Organizes the information in a clear order, here a sequence from seed to weigh-off (W.6.2)","Develops the topic with relevant facts and details from the fact box, in the student’s own sentences (W.6.2)","Uses transitions such as first, after that, as a result, and precise topic words such as pollen, vine, weigh-off (W.6.2)","Keeps a formal style and ends with a concluding statement that follows from the explanation (W.6.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"narrative",label:"Story",icon:"📖",hint:"Two friends, one fork in the maze"},std:["W.6.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative with a clear sequence, dialogue, and descriptive details",html:()=>`<p class="big">Halfway through the corn maze, two friends reach a fork. Each one is sure a different path is right. Write the scene. It can be real or imagined. Write 5 to 8 sentences.</p>${pills(["Set the scene","The fork","What they say and do","How it ends"])}${checklist(["Show who is there and where they are","Tell what happens in order","Let the characters talk","Use time words and exact details the reader can see, hear, or feel","End in a way that fits the story"])}`,key:["Sets up the context and introduces a narrator or characters (W.6.3)","Events unfold in a natural, logical sequence (W.6.3)","Uses dialogue, pacing, or description to develop the events or the characters (W.6.3)","Uses transition words for time or place, precise words, and sensory details (W.6.3)","Provides a conclusion that follows from the events (W.6.3)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Field Plans",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.1"],dur:170,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer with its units.",desc:"area of a triangle, a trapezoid, and a cut-corner field, and comparing two plans",fig:()=>fieldsFig(),qs:["Field A is a triangle. Its height is drawn outside the triangle. Find its area.","Find the area of Field B. Tell how you broke it into smaller shapes.","Field C is a rectangle with one corner cut off for parking. Find the area of Field C.","Which plan is better? Plan 1 is a triangle with a base of 24 meters and a height of 15 meters. Plan 2 is a parallelogram with a base of 16 meters and a height of 11 meters. Which plan has more planting area? How much more?"],key:["40 square meters. 1/2 × 10 × 8 = 40. The height can be outside the triangle. Or: 10 × 8 = 80, then halve. (6.G.A.1, triangle)","300 square meters. Rectangle 20 × 12 = 240. Triangle 1/2 × 10 × 12 = 60. 240 + 60 = 300. Or: the trapezoid rule, 1/2 × (20 + 30) × 12 = 300. Partial: the rectangle alone, 240, is approaching. (6.G.A.1, decomposing a trapezoid)","960 square meters. Whole rectangle 40 × 25 = 1,000. Cut corner 1/2 × 10 × 8 = 40. 1,000 − 40 = 960. Or: a 30 by 25 rectangle, 750, plus the 10 by 25 end less the cut corner, 250 − 40 = 210. Partial: 1,000 leaves the corner on and does not count. (6.G.A.1, composing into a rectangle)","Plan 1, by 4 square meters. Plan 1: 1/2 × 24 × 15 = 180. Plan 2: 16 × 11 = 176. Partial: both areas right with no difference given is approaching. (6.G.A.1, real-world problem)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Fact Check",parts:[{std:["W.6.8"],label:"checks met",dur:115,dir:"Question: How fast does a giant pumpkin grow? Say 1 and 2 out loud. Write 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, spotting plagiarism, paraphrasing, and listing source information",html:()=>`<div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px">${card("Source 1",SRC1_CARD)}${card("Source 2","Web page: “Mega Grow Plant Food: Grow a 1,000-Pound Pumpkin in Weeks, Guaranteed!” From a company that sells plant food. No author. No date.")}${card("Source 3","Forum post by user gourdfan88, 2014: “Somebody told me pumpkins can grow 100 pounds a day.”")}</div>${srcBox()}<div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${card("Student note A","A giant pumpkin can gain 30 to 40 pounds in a single day at the peak of summer.")}${card("Student note B","In midsummer, the biggest pumpkins can add up to 40 pounds every day (Delgado).")}${card("Student note C","Delgado writes that a giant pumpkin “can gain 30 to 40 pounds in a single day.”")}</div>`,qs:["Source 1 and Source 3 give different numbers. Which number would you use in a report? Give two reasons why that source is more credible.","Three students took notes from Source 1. Which note is plagiarism? How could that student fix it?","Paraphrase the second sentence in the Source 1 box, the one about water and shade. Use your own words, and give the author credit.","List Source 1 the way you would at the end of a report: author, title, who published it, and the year."],key:["The number from Source 1, 30 to 40 pounds a day. Two reasons, such as: it names an author with a farm job, it comes from an extension office, it is recent, it is written to teach. Source 3 is an unnamed user repeating what somebody said in 2014. (W.6.8, credibility)","Note A. It copies the source almost word for word with no quotation marks and no credit. Fix: put the copied words in quotation marks and name Delgado, or rewrite it in new words and name Delgado. Note B is a fair paraphrase and Note C is a correct quote. (W.6.8, avoiding plagiarism)","A true paraphrase with credit, such as: Delgado explains that growers must water every day and keep the pumpkin shaded so its skin stays soft and does not split. (W.6.8, paraphrase with credit)","Anita Delgado, “Growing Giants: A Guide for First-Time Growers,” Marlow County Extension Office, 2025. Any order is fine when all four pieces are there. (W.6.8, basic bibliographic information)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Price It",parts:[{std:["SL.6.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:90,dir:"Plan for 30 seconds. Then talk for 45 to 60 seconds. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"presenting a claim and findings out loud in a logical order",html:()=>`<p class="big">Should the farm stand sell pumpkins by the pound or for one price each? Make a claim. Back it up with findings from the table.</p>${F.table(["","Saturday: sold by the pound","Sunday: one price each"],[["<strong>Price</strong>","$0.60 a pound","$8 each, any size"],["<strong>Pumpkins sold</strong>","120","150"],["<strong>Money taken in</strong>","$1,080","$1,200"],["<strong>What workers saw</strong>","A long line at the scale","The biggest pumpkins were gone by noon"]],{caption:"Findings from one weekend"})}${pills(["My claim","Finding 1","Finding 2","So..."])}`,key:["States a clear claim: by the pound or one price (SL.6.4)","Puts ideas in a logical order: claim, findings, closing (SL.6.4)","Uses pertinent facts and details from the table, such as $120 more and 30 more pumpkins on Sunday, or about $9 a pumpkin on Saturday against $8 on Sunday (SL.6.4)","Keeps eye contact with the camera (SL.6.4)","Speaks with adequate volume and clear pronunciation for 45 to 60 seconds (SL.6.4)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Two Writers, One Maze",short:"Two Writers",parts:[{std:["RI.6.6","RI.6.9"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the short piece by the farm owner. The maze article is in the box under it. Then answer out loud.",desc:"an author's purpose and point of view, and how two authors present the same work differently",html:()=>`${passage(OWNER,OWNER_TITLE)}<details class="look-back"><summary>Look back at the maze article</summary><div class="passage sm">${paras(INFO)}</div></details>`,key:["Ask: What is this writer's purpose, and how can you tell? (To explain and defend why the farm cuts a maze into good corn. First person throughout, a question other people ask answered head on, and money reasons given.) (RI.6.6)","Ask: Which sentences show the writer's point of view about the trampled stalks? (We do not mind, and The crop we are growing in October is not corn. It is a reason for families to drive out here.) (RI.6.6)","Ask: Both pieces are about the same maze. How is the way each writer presents it different? (The article explains the process from the outside, step by step and without opinion: the computer drawing, the crisscross planting, the GPS tractor. The owner writes from inside the farm, in first person, and argues about money and the reason for doing it at all.) (RI.6.9)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Make the Case",short:"Argument",parts:[{std:["W.6.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short argument with a claim, reasons, evidence, and a closing",html:()=>`<p class="big">The farm can open the maze on Saturdays only, or on both Saturdays and Sundays. Which should it do?</p><p class="big">Write 4 or 5 sentences: state your claim, give two reasons, back at least one reason with evidence from the table, and close.</p>${F.table(["","Saturday","Sunday"],[["<strong>Visitors</strong>","940","300"],["<strong>Workers needed</strong>","9","9"],["<strong>Hours open</strong>","8","6"]],{caption:"The weekend of October 18 at the maze"})}`,key:["States a clear claim, such as: The farm should open the maze on Saturdays only. (W.6.1)","Supports the claim with two reasons, not one reason said twice. (W.6.1)","Backs at least one reason with evidence from the table, such as 940 visitors on Saturday against 300 on Sunday, or the same 9 workers needed either day. (W.6.1)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the argument. (W.6.1)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Read the Gate Count",short:"Gate Count",parts:[{std:["SL.6.2"],n:3,dur:50,read:GATE_READ,dir:"Look at the table. Then listen to your teacher. Answer out loud.",desc:"putting information from a table together with information given out loud",fig:()=>F.table(["Weekend","Visitors"],[["Oct 4","410"],["Oct 11","680"],["Oct 18","1,240"],["Oct 25","1,515"]],{caption:"Maze visitors by weekend"}),key:["Read the two sentences out loud once, then ask the questions.","Ask: What does the table show about October? (Visitors climb every weekend, from 410 up to 1,515, almost four times as many by the end of the month.) (SL.6.2)","Ask: Put the table together with what I read. Does the money from the last weekend beat the 9,000 dollars the corn would bring? Show me. (Yes. 1,515 visitors at 12 dollars is 18,180 dollars, which is about twice 9,000.) (SL.6.2)","Ask: What does the table tell you that the spoken facts do not? (How the crowd changes week by week. The spoken facts give only a price and a yearly corn total.) (SL.6.2)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Water and Weather",short:"Signed Numbers",parts:[{title:"Tank Shares",std:["6.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer and how you know.",desc:"dividing a fraction by a fraction in context and by computation",qs:["Kiona has 3/4 of a tank of water left. Each vine takes 1/8 of a tank. How many vines can she water?","Say what 3/4 divided by 1/8 means in that story.","Find 2/3 divided by 4/9."],key:["6 vines. 3/4 = 6/8, and 6/8 holds six 1/8 pieces. Or: 3/4 × 8/1 = 6. (6.NS.A.1)","It asks how many 1/8 tank shares fit inside 3/4 of a tank. Partial: an answer about sharing 3/4 between 8 vines shows the two meanings of division are mixed and is approaching. (6.NS.A.1, interpreting the quotient)","3/2, or 1 1/2. 2/3 x 9/4 = 18/12 = 3/2. Or: common denominators, 6/9 ÷ 4/9 = 6 ÷ 4 = 3/2. (6.NS.A.1)"]},{title:"Above and Below",std:["6.NS.C.5"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Say what the numbers mean.",desc:"using positive and negative numbers for opposite directions, and explaining what 0 means",qs:["The night thermometer in the patch reads negative 3 degrees Celsius. What does 0 stand for on that scale?","Kiona's log shows plus 32 pounds one week and negative 5 pounds another week. What does negative 5 mean?","Which temperature is colder, negative 8 degrees or 6 degrees? Which one is farther from 0?"],key:["0 is the freezing point of water. Below it the reading is negative, above it the reading is positive. Partial: naming 0 as freezing with no account of the two directions is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)","The pumpkin lost 5 pounds that week. The plus and the minus point in opposite directions: gain and loss. Partial: lost 5 pounds with no account of the two opposite directions is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)","Negative 8 is colder. Negative 8 is also farther from 0, because it is 8 away and 6 is only 6 away. Partial: one of the two questions right is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Crates and Formulas",short:"Formulas",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.2","6.EE.A.2c"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up or tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"volume of a prism with a fractional edge length, and evaluating two formulas",qs:["A seed crate measures 3 1/2 feet by 2 feet by 1 1/2 feet. Use V = l x w x h to find its volume.","Evaluate 3x squared when x = 4.","A cube-shaped bin has edges of 5 feet. Use the formula A = 6s squared to find its surface area."],key:["10 1/2 cubic feet, or 10.5. 3.5 x 2 = 7, and 7 x 1.5 = 10.5. The unit has to be cubic feet. Or: 7/2 × 2 × 3/2 = 42/4 = 10 1/2. (6.G.A.2)","48. Square the 4 first to get 16, then multiply by 3. Answering 144 means the student multiplied before squaring. (6.EE.A.2c)","150 square feet. 5 squared is 25, and 25 x 6 = 150. Or: count six faces of 25 square feet each. (6.EE.A.2c)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Gate Math",short:"Gate Math",parts:[{title:"Two Changing Numbers",std:["6.EE.C.9"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud. You can write on your paper.",desc:"writing an equation for two related quantities and naming the independent and dependent variable",fig:()=>F.table(["p (people)","c (cost in dollars)"],[["1","12"],["3","?"],["5","?"],["10","?"]],{caption:"Maze tickets at 12 dollars a person"}),qs:["Write an equation for the total cost c of tickets for p people.","Which variable is independent and which is dependent? How do you know?","Fill in the three missing costs in the table."],key:["c = 12p. Or: c = p × 12 or 12p = c count equally. Partial: p = 12c is the reversal and does not count. (6.EE.C.9)","p is independent, because you pick how many people. c is dependent, because the cost follows from p. Partial: naming both variables with no reason is approaching. (6.EE.C.9)","36, 60, and 120 dollars. Partial: two of the three right is approaching. (6.EE.C.9)"]},{title:"Weigh-Off Numbers",std:["6.SP.B.5"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the list of weights. Answer out loud.",desc:"summarizing a data set: number of observations, what was measured, median, and range",fig:()=>F.table(["Weights at the weigh-off, in pounds, in order"],[["96, 150, 150, 215, 487, 540, 612"]],{caption:"Marlow County weigh-off"}),qs:["How many pumpkins are in this data set, and what was measured about each one?","What is the median weight?","What is the range, and what does it tell you about the day?"],key:["7 pumpkins, and the measurement is each pumpkin's weight in pounds. Partial: the count right with nothing said about what was measured is approaching. (6.SP.B.5)","215 pounds. It is the fourth of the seven weights, with three below and three above. Or: count in from both ends until the two counts meet. (6.SP.B.5)","516 pounds, from 612 - 96. The weights are spread very wide, so a few giants sat beside some much smaller pumpkins. Partial: 516 with no account of what it tells you about the day is approaching. (6.SP.B.5)"]}]},{id:20,title:"How a Vine Works",short:"The Vine",parts:[{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"relating structures of a vine to their functions and explaining where plant mass comes from",html:()=>`<p class="big">A pumpkin vine thirty feet long grows from one seed in a single season. It has broad leaves, hollow stems, shallow spreading roots and curling tendrils that grip whatever they touch.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Give the function of the broad leaves, the shallow spreading roots and the tendrils, one each.","A single seed becomes a vine with dozens of kilograms of fruit. Where does nearly all that extra material come from?","The underside of a leaf has tiny openings that can open and close. What has to pass through them, and what is the cost of holding them open?"],key:["Broad leaves catch as much light as possible for photosynthesis. Shallow spreading roots take up water and minerals from a wide area of surface soil. Tendrils grip to anchor and position the vine. Partial: two of the three functions given is approaching, and the tendrils are usually the ones left without a job. (LS1)","From carbon dioxide in the air and water, which the plant builds into sugars and then into stem, leaf and fruit tissue. Soil minerals are needed but make up a small part of the mass. Partial: answering that the material comes from the soil is approaching, since minerals do come from there, but nearly all the mass comes from air and water. (LS1)","Carbon dioxide has to get in and oxygen out for photosynthesis. The cost is water vapor escaping while they are open, so a plant in dry heat must balance taking in gas against losing water. Partial: naming the gases that pass through with nothing said about water vapor escaping while the openings are held open is approaching. (LS1)"]},{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting growth data and explaining how a plant responds to its conditions",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four vines were measured at the same date. All were planted on the same day in the same patch.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Vine</th><th>Hours of sun a day</th><th>Water</th><th>Fruit set</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>9</td><td>regular</td><td>4 pumpkins</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>9</td><td>sparse</td><td>1 pumpkin</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>regular</td><td>1 pumpkin</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>sparse</td><td>none</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sun",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("pumpkin",74)}</div>`,qs:["What does this chart show about how sun and water each affect fruit production?","Vine 2 had plenty of sun and still set only one pumpkin. Explain why water matters even when light is plentiful.","Bees visit the flowers of these vines. What does the plant get from that visit, and what happens to the flowers that no bee visits?"],key:["Both matter and neither is enough alone: full sun with regular water gave four, taking away either one dropped it to one, and taking away both gave none. Partial: saying both sun and water matter, with no row of the chart used to show that neither is enough on its own, is approaching. (LS1)","Water is a raw material for photosynthesis and the way minerals move through the plant, and it fills the cells that hold a leaf open. Without enough, the leaf cannot make sugar at full speed no matter how much light falls on it. Partial: saying the vine needs water to live, with no role named for water in making sugar or moving minerals, is approaching. (LS1)","The plant gets pollen carried from one flower to another so that seeds and fruit can form. Unvisited flowers are not pollinated, so they drop off without making a pumpkin. Partial: naming the pollination with nothing said about unvisited flowers dropping off without a pumpkin is approaching. (LS1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Seeds and Surprises",short:"Seeds",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining variation among offspring and the role of two parents",html:()=>`<p class="big">Seeds saved from one large orange pumpkin were planted the next spring. The fruit they produced was recorded.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fruit from those seeds</th><th>How many</th></tr>
     <tr><td>large and orange, like the parent</td><td>5</td></tr>
     <tr><td>small and orange</td><td>7</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pale yellow, medium</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lumpy green</td><td>2</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["All these seeds came from one pumpkin. Explain why the fruit they produced was so varied.","Pale yellow and green fruit appeared although the parent was orange. How can a trait show up in offspring that the parent did not show?","A grower wants mostly large orange fruit next year. Which seeds should be saved, and why will the result still not be uniform?"],key:["Each seed was formed from pollen that could come from a different plant, so each carries a different combination of gene versions from two parents. One fruit can hold many different sets of instructions. Partial: saying the seeds all turned out different, with nothing said about each one carrying a combination from two parents, is approaching. (LS3)","The parent carried gene versions it did not display, because another version was expressed instead. Offspring that receive two copies of the hidden version show the trait. Partial: saying the trait skipped a generation, with nothing said about the parent carrying a version it did not display, is approaching. (LS3)","Seeds from the largest orange fruit, because those plants carry gene versions for size and color. The result still varies because each seed gets a new combination and pollen from other plants can carry different versions. Partial: naming the seeds to save with nothing said about why the result will still vary is approaching. (LS3)"]},{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"distinguishing inherited from acquired traits and describing where variation comes from",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four pumpkins in the patch are described. One has a flat side where it rested against a board. One is ribbed deeply all around. One is pale because it grew under leaves. One has a warty skin like none of its neighbors.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("board",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which of these four features could be passed to the next generation through seed, and which could not? Explain your sorting.","The warty skin appeared in a patch where no plant had it before. Name the kind of change that can produce a new trait like that.","Why does a population of plants with many different traits have an advantage over a population where every plant is identical?"],key:["Deep ribbing and warty skin could be passed on, because they come from the plant’s genes. The flat side and the pale color could not, because they were caused by a board and by shade during that fruit’s life. Partial: three of the four sorted correctly is approaching, and the pale color is usually the one put on the wrong side. (LS3)","A mutation, a change in the genetic instructions, which can produce a trait that was not present in the parents. It can also arrive in pollen from a plant elsewhere. Partial: describing it as a new or odd trait without naming it a mutation is approaching. (LS3)","If conditions change, such as a new disease, a dry year or a cold spell, some individuals in a varied population are likely to have traits that let them survive and reproduce. In an identical population every plant meets the change the same way. Partial: saying variety is better, with no change in conditions named that the varied population could survive, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Rain, Runoff and Rock",short:"Runoff",parts:[{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining infiltration, runoff and the movement of water through a field",html:()=>`<p class="big">The same amount of rain fell on three parts of the patch, and the water that ran off into the ditch was measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Water that ran off</th></tr>
     <tr><td>loose tilled soil</td><td>a little</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bare packed path</td><td>most of it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>soil under thick vine leaves</td><td>very little</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("rain",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why a packed path sheds water while loose soil absorbs it.","Water that soaks into the patch does not stay where it landed. Name two places it can end up.","The ditch water is brown after a storm. What is in it, where did it come from, and why does losing it matter to a grower?"],key:["Packed soil has little pore space, so there is nowhere for water to go and it flows across the surface. Loose soil has many connected spaces, so water infiltrates. Plant cover also slows water and gives it time to soak in. Partial: saying packed soil is too hard for water to get into, with nothing said about the pore space loose soil has, is approaching. (ESS2)","Any two: taken up by roots and released from leaves, moving down to become groundwater, moving sideways through soil toward a stream, or evaporating from the surface. Partial: one destination named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS2)","Soil carried off the surface by running water, which is erosion. It matters because topsoil holds the nutrients and the water holding structure the crop depends on, and it takes a very long time to replace. Partial: naming the soil in the water with nothing said about why losing topsoil matters to the crop is approaching. (ESS2)"]},{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the rock and soil cycle and the energy that drives surface change",html:()=>`<p class="big">A stone wall at the edge of the patch is made of rounded cobbles that came out of the field. The field soil is dark and crumbly. A gravel bank sits where an old stream once ran.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["The cobbles are rounded rather than sharp. What process shaped them, and what does that tell you about where they have been?","Field soil is dark and crumbly while the gravel bank is pale and loose. What makes soil different from broken rock?","Name the two sources of energy that drive the processes moving rock and soil around on Earth’s surface."],key:["Tumbling in moving water wore the edges off, so they traveled in a stream or were carried by ice and meltwater before ending up in this field. Rounded shape records transport. Partial: saying water wore the edges off, with nothing said about the rounding being a record of having traveled, is approaching. (ESS2)","Soil contains decayed plant and animal material, the organic part, mixed with broken rock, and it holds water and supports life. Gravel is rock pieces with almost none of that. Partial: saying soil is finer than gravel, with nothing said about the decayed plant and animal material in it, is approaching. (ESS2)","The sun, which drives the water cycle, wind and weather, and Earth’s internal energy, which drives the movement of plates, uplift and volcanic activity. Gravity then pulls material downhill. Partial: naming the sun alone, with nothing said about Earth's internal energy, is approaching, and gravity on its own is not one of the two sources. (ESS2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Dry Years and Hard Choices",short:"Dry Years",parts:[{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing water resources, drought data and the limits on a renewable supply",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rainfall and the depth to water in the farm well were recorded for four years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Rain for the season</th><th>Depth to water in the well</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>58 cm</td><td>9 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>31 cm</td><td>12 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>26 cm</td><td>16 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>55 cm</td><td>14 m</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cloud",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe the relationship between the rainfall and the depth to water in the well.","Year 4 had nearly as much rain as year 1, and yet the water is still 5 meters deeper than it was. Give a likely reason.","Name two things this farm could do to use less water, and say which one you would try first and why."],key:["As rainfall falls the water level drops, meaning the depth to water increases, and when rain returns the level partly recovers. Rainfall refills the underground supply. Partial: saying less rain means less water, with nothing said about the depth to water increasing as the level drops, is approaching. (ESS3)","Refilling is slow, so one good year does not replace what three years of pumping and low rain removed. Pumping may also have continued at the same rate, so withdrawals still outran the recharge. Partial: saying one year is not enough, with nothing said about refilling being slower than the pumping, is approaching. (ESS3)","Any two: drip irrigation, watering at night, mulching to cut evaporation, planting a crop that needs less water, or catching and storing rainfall. The reason should name cost, effort or how much water it saves. Partial: two measures named with no reason given for choosing one of them first is approaching. (ESS3)"]},{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"evaluating human impact on soil and the use of data in making a decision",html:()=>`<p class="big">A neighboring field has been plowed bare every autumn for twenty years. The grower who owns the pumpkin patch plants a cover crop of rye instead and mows it in spring.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("tractor",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name two effects on the soil of leaving a field bare through the wet season.","How does a cover crop reduce those effects? Explain the mechanism, not just the result.","A grower says a cover crop is a waste because it is not sold. What evidence would you gather over several years to test that claim?"],key:["Any two: topsoil washed or blown away, nutrients leached out by rain passing through, soil compacted and crusted, and less organic matter so the soil holds less water. Partial: one effect named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS3)","Living roots hold soil particles in place and keep pore spaces open, leaves intercept rain so it hits the ground with less force, roots take up nutrients that would otherwise wash away, and mowed growth adds organic matter as it breaks down. Partial: saying the cover crop protects the soil, with no mechanism named, is approaching, which is the answer this question is written to catch. (ESS3)","Measure the two fields over several seasons: soil lost to the ditch, soil organic matter, water held after a rain, fertilizer needed, and crop yield. The claim is testable because all of those can be compared with the cost of seed. Partial: naming yield alone, with nothing measured about the soil itself, is approaching. (ESS3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Heat in the Patch",short:"Heat",parts:[{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining thermal energy transfer, temperature and the energy stored in a sample",html:()=>`<p class="big">Temperatures were taken at noon on a cool autumn day.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>Temperature</th></tr>
     <tr><td>air above the patch</td><td>14 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>inside the compost pile</td><td>46 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>soil under black plastic</td><td>24 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bare soil</td><td>15 °C</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}</div>`,qs:["Soil under black plastic is 9 degrees warmer than bare soil. Explain the transfer that causes the difference.","The compost pile is far warmer than the air around it on a cool day. Where is that energy coming from?","A bucket of soil at 24 degrees and a bucket at 15 degrees are both brought indoors. Which transfers more energy to the room, and why?"],key:["Black absorbs more of the sun’s radiation than pale bare soil reflects back, and the plastic also traps warm air and slows the escape of thermal energy, so energy accumulates in the soil beneath. Partial: saying black absorbs more sunlight, with nothing said about the plastic also slowing the escape of energy, is approaching. (PS3)","From the material breaking down inside the pile: energy stored in the plant matter is released by the organisms decomposing it, which raises the temperature of the pile from the inside. Partial: saying the pile makes its own heat, with nothing said about energy stored in the plant matter being released as it is broken down, is approaching. (PS3)","The warmer bucket, because energy moves from warmer to cooler until temperatures even out, so the 24 degree soil has more energy to transfer before it matches the room. Mass matters too, which is why the buckets are the same size. Partial: choosing the warmer bucket with nothing said about energy moving from warmer to cooler until the temperatures even out is approaching. (PS3)"]},{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. You may use your paper.",desc:"relating kinetic and potential energy to mass, speed and height in a loaded cart",html:()=>`<p class="big">A wheelbarrow is loaded at the top of the sloping path and rolled down to the barn.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["An empty wheelbarrow and a loaded one sit at the top of the same path. Which has more stored energy, and why?","A loaded wheelbarrow rolling at 4 kilometers an hour is pushed to 8. Does its kinetic energy double, or more than double? Explain.","The loaded wheelbarrow is harder to stop than the empty one at the same speed. Explain that using energy."],key:["The loaded one. Gravitational potential energy depends on mass as well as height, and both are at the same height, so the greater mass stores more. Partial: choosing the loaded wheelbarrow because it is heavier, with nothing said about both being at the same height, is approaching. (PS3)","More than double, four times as much, because kinetic energy depends on the square of the speed. Partial: answering more than double without the figure of four times, or four times with no reason from the squared speed, is approaching. (PS3)","It has more kinetic energy at that speed because it has more mass, and stopping it means transferring all of that energy away through friction in the brakes, the wheels and the ground, which takes more work or more distance. Partial: saying it is heavier so it is harder to stop, with nothing said about the energy having to be transferred away, is approaching. (PS3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"A Crate That Holds",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. Write your criteria and constraints on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem with measurable criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Pumpkins are bruising on the wagon ride from the patch to the stand. Available: cardboard, straw, old blankets, scrap wood, rope. The crates must be carried by one person, must stack, and must be built from what is there before the weekend.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, naming the need rather than a device.","List two criteria you could measure and two constraints taken from the description.","One student writes the criterion as make a good crate. What is wrong with that as a criterion, and how would you fix it?"],key:["Something like: carry pumpkins from the patch to the stand without bruising them. Partial: a sentence that names a crate or a wagon rather than the result to be achieved is approaching. (ETS1)","Criteria: number of bruised pumpkins per load, how many stack safely, how fast a load can be packed. Constraints: only the materials listed, one person must be able to carry it, must stack, ready before the weekend. Partial: two criteria and one constraint, or one of each, is approaching. (ETS1)","Good cannot be measured, so two people could disagree about whether it was met and no test could settle it. Fix it by naming a number, such as no more than one bruised pumpkin in a load of twenty. Partial: saying it is too vague, with no measurable version offered in its place, is approaching. (ETS1)"]},{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing tested designs, identifying the best features and planning the next test",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three crates were tested with twenty pumpkins each over the same rough track.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Bruised</th><th>Stacks</th><th>Weight when full</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: wood, no padding</td><td>6</td><td>yes</td><td>heavy</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: cardboard with straw</td><td>1</td><td>no, it sags</td><td>light</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: wood lined with blanket</td><td>2</td><td>yes</td><td>heavy</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which crate meets the most criteria and constraints? Support your choice with the numbers.","Crate B bruised the fewest and is still not the answer. Which requirement does it fail, and why does that matter on a wagon?","Name the best feature of each crate, then describe one combined design you would test next."],key:["Crate C. Two bruised out of twenty, it stacks, and the only drawback is weight, while A bruises six and B does not stack. Partial: naming Crate C with no number from the table used to support it is approaching. (ETS1)","It does not stack, because the cardboard sags under load. On a wagon, crates that cannot be stacked waste space and can shift and tip, so the constraint matters as much as the bruising. Partial: naming the stacking failure with nothing said about why that matters on a loaded wagon is approaching. (ETS1)","A: strong stackable frame. B: effective straw padding and light weight. C: padding plus a stackable frame. A combined design would be a light wooden frame lined with straw, tested the same way to see whether bruising stays near one and the weight drops. Partial: a best feature given for each crate with no combined design described is approaching. (ETS1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"What the Ground Kept",short:"The Ground",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the dig notes. Answer out loud.",desc:"drawing conclusions about contact, timing and the limits of evidence from a dig",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three notes from a dig:</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Note</th><th>What the diggers wrote</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>Pots here carry an unusual wavy pattern. Pots with the same pattern were found at a village twenty five miles away.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>A thick layer of river sand lies between two layers of house floors.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>Storage pits in the upper layer hold charred seeds of squash, beans and grain.</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("pan",74)}</div>`,qs:["Two villages twenty five miles apart made pots with the same unusual pattern. Name two different explanations that could account for that.","A layer of river sand sits between two layers of house floors. What happened at this spot, and in what order?","The pits hold seeds of three different crops. What does growing several crops rather than one do for a farming village?"],key:["Any two of: the two villages traded pots, people moved between them and brought the style, they learned the style from the same source, or they are the same people using two sites. Two explanations is the point, since one find rarely has one explanation. Partial: one explanation offered is approaching, since the question asks for two. (HSS-6.1)","People lived there, a river flooded and left sand over their floors, then people built and lived there again on top of the sand. Order matters: lower is earlier. Partial: the three events named out of order is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","Spreads the risk. If one crop fails, the others may not, and different crops ripen at different times, so the food supply is steadier through the year. Partial: saying more crops means more food, with nothing about spreading risk, is approaching. (HSS-6.1)"]},{std:["HSS-6.2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing writing, temple economies and standard measures in Mesopotamia, Egypt and Kush",html:()=>`<p class="big">Most surviving clay tablets from Mesopotamian cities are not stories. They are counts: so many measures of barley in, so many out, so many owed. Scribes trained for years to write them. At the center of such a city stood a stepped tower called a ziggurat, and the temple beside it stored grain, owned land and lent out seed. Egyptian officials counted the harvest in standard measures of grain.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("board",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would a city that collects and hands out grain need writing before it needs stories?","The temple at the center of the city also stored grain, held land and lent seed. What does that tell you about how religion and the economy fit together there?","Egypt counted the harvest in standard measures. What can a government do with a standard measure that it cannot do without one?"],key:["Because records of who owed and received what are too many and too exact to hold in memory, and they have to outlast the person who made them. Accounting is the problem writing was first used to solve. Partial: saying writing is useful with no account of records outlasting memory is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","They were not separate. The temple was a major landholder, lender and storehouse, so religious authority and economic power sat in the same institution and priests held real economic influence. Partial: treating religion and the economy as separate is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","Compare, add and tax fairly across the whole country, plan for a bad year, and check whether an official handed over everything collected. Without one, every district’s numbers mean something different. Partial: one use of a shared measure named is approaching. (HSS-6.2)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Who Settles the Dispute",short:"Disputes",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Hebrew hill farming, leadership before the kings, and law tied to belief",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Ancient Hebrews farmed hill country, cutting flat steps called terraces into the slopes and catching rainwater in plastered pits. Before they had kings, disputes between families and tribes were settled by leaders called judges, who held that position through standing and reputation rather than by inheriting it. Their religion, Judaism, taught belief in one God, and the law they judged by came from the Torah.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["Cutting terraces into a hillside is heavy work that takes years. Why would farmers do it instead of farming the flat land?","Judges were not kings and did not pass the position to their children. How is that political structure different from a kingdom?","The Torah and the belief in one God were held to be one set of teachings for the whole people. How would that help hold together tribes with no single strong ruler?"],key:["Terraces stop rain from washing the soil down the slope and hold water where roots can reach it, which makes steep land farmable. The flat, well watered land was held by other, stronger peoples, so the hills were what they had. Partial: one of the terracing and the reason they farmed hills given is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","Authority rested on the person rather than on a family line, it was recognized rather than inherited, and it was usually local and temporary rather than a permanent government over everyone. Partial: one feature of that authority named is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","It gave separate tribes one shared standard and one shared story of who they were, so they could settle disputes by the same rules and act together without a central government forcing it. Partial: naming the shared law with nothing said about acting together without a central government is approaching. (HSS-6.3)"]},{std:["HSS-6.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Greek public festivals, stories about the gods, and separate city-state calendars",html:()=>`<p class="big">Once a year Athens held a festival at which playwrights competed and most of the city came to watch the plays, with wealthy citizens paying the costs. Greek religious belief included gods connected to grain and the growing year, and the stories told about them explained why the seasons changed. Each city-state kept its own calendar, with its own month names and its own festival days.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("palette",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["Wealthy citizens paid for the plays and most of the city attended. What does that arrangement tell you about Greek public life?","Greek stories about the gods explained why the seasons changed. What does it tell you about a society when its religious stories carry the explanation of the natural world?","Each city-state had its own calendar and month names. What practical problem would that create for Greeks from different cities?"],key:["Public culture was a civic duty and a shared event rather than private entertainment: the rich gained standing by funding it, and the audience was the citizen body, so theater was part of city business. Partial: saying people enjoyed the plays with nothing said about civic duty is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Belief did the work that science does now, so religion was woven into farming, the calendar and ordinary planning rather than set aside from them. The stories were accepted explanations, not only entertainment. Partial: calling the stories entertainment only is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Agreeing on a date. Traders, allies and travelers could not name one day the same way, which makes arranging meetings, markets or joint action harder. Partial: saying calendars differed with no consequence named is approaching. (HSS-6.4)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Stores, Dikes and Tables",short:"Stores",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing central grain storage, inherited occupation and orally kept teachings in early India",html:()=>`<p class="big">Large brick platforms stood in Indus cities, which many archaeologists read as the floors of granaries. In later Indian society, the ranked group a person was born into set what work that person could do, and Hinduism taught that each group had its own dharma, or duty. Buddhism, which also began in India, drew followers from every one of those groups. The early Hindu teachings, the Vedas, were memorized and recited word for word for many generations before anyone wrote them down.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("ear",74)}</div>`,qs:["If a city stored grain in one central place, who would control that store, and why does that matter?","Being born into a group decided a person’s work, and Hinduism taught that each group had its own duty. Name one effect on a single person’s life and one effect on the economy as a whole.","The early Hindu teachings were passed on by memory for generations. Name one strength and one risk of keeping knowledge that way."],key:["Whoever ran the city: rulers, officials or priests. Control of stored food is power, because it decides who eats in a bad year and who owes the store afterward. Partial: naming who held the store with nothing said about why that is power is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","For the person: no choice of work however able they were, status fixed at birth, and a duty attached to that status. For the economy: skills stayed in families and were passed down reliably, but talent was wasted and work could not shift easily to where it was needed. Partial: one of the person and the economy answered is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","Strength: memorized text travels with people, needs no materials, and reciters checked each other so wording stayed remarkably stable. Risk: nothing is left if a generation of reciters is lost, and there is no copy to check a disputed line against. Partial: one of the strength and the risk given is approaching. (HSS-6.5)"]},{std:["HSS-6.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the mandate of heaven, state granaries and Confucian duty in China",html:()=>`<p class="big">Chinese rulers held that heaven approved a ruler who governed well. A flood or a famine could be read as a sign that the approval had been lost. Han officials kept state granaries that bought grain when it was cheap and sold it cheaply when harvests were poor. Confucianism, the teaching of Confucius, held that society held together when each person met the duties of their own role.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["If people read a flood or famine as a sign a ruler had lost heaven’s approval, what pressure does that put on a Chinese government?","State granaries bought grain cheap and sold it cheap in bad years. What economic problem was that meant to solve?","How would the Confucian teaching that each person should meet the duties of their role be useful to a government?"],key:["Real pressure to control rivers, store grain and relieve hunger, because a disaster handled badly becomes an argument that the ruler should be replaced. The belief turned flood control into a political necessity. Partial: naming the belief with no practical pressure on the ruler named is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","Wild swings in the price of food. Buying in good years kept the price from collapsing on farmers, and selling in bad years kept it from rising out of reach, so the government steadied both supply and price. Partial: one of the two directions of the price swing explained is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","It asks people to accept their place and the obligations that come with it, including obedience upward, which supports order without constant force. Note that Confucianism also placed duties on rulers toward the people. Partial: describing obedience with nothing said about the duties on rulers is approaching. (HSS-6.6)"]},{std:["HSS-6.7"],n:3,dur:120,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Roman posted law, the grain handout and household religion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rome’s first written laws, the Twelve Tables, were carved and set up in the forum. Later the government handed out free or cheap grain to poor citizens in the city. Romans honored small household gods at home with daily offerings, alongside the state gods of the public temples. Christianity began in the Roman province of Judea with the teachings of Jesus of Nazareth and spread through the empire. Because it did not honor those gods, Roman law outlawed the practice and persecuted Christians who would not give it up, taking their property, sending some into exile and putting some to death, until the emperor Constantine legalized it in 313 and it later became the religion of the state.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("board",74)}${ico("bread",74)}${ico("lamp",74)}</div>`,qs:["Who gains the most when law is carved and set up where anyone can read it?","The government gave out grain to poor citizens in the city. What does that policy tell you about how politically important the city crowd was?","Romans honored household gods at home as well as state gods in public temples. What does that tell you about where religion sat in Roman life, and according to the note, how did Rome's treatment of Christianity change over time?"],key:["Ordinary people, the plebeians, who can now see the rule instead of being told what it is by an official from a wealthy family. Posted law limits private interpretation. Partial: naming the plebeians with nothing said about private interpretation is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","Very important. A government does not feed a group it can ignore, so the city population had enough political weight, by votes, by support for leaders or by its sheer presence in the capital, that keeping it fed was worth the cost. Partial: saying the city mattered with no account of its political weight is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","It ran through both private and public life rather than sitting in one building. Religion was part of ordinary household routine and also an official state duty, so the two levels supported each other. On Christianity: it began in Judea, and because it did not honor the Roman gods the law outlawed the practice and persecuted Christians who would not give it up, until Constantine legalized it in 313 and it later became the state religion. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the household and state halves with nothing on Christianity sits there. (HSS-6.7)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Sound Check",std:["RL.7.4"],needs:["RL.6.4"],n:2,dir:"Read the poem out loud. Then answer out loud.",html:()=>`<div class="figure" style="align-items:flex-start"><div><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${POEM_TITLE}</h3>${poemHtml()}</div></div><ol class="qlist"><li>Look at the last stanza. Which two line endings rhyme? The poem’s final rhyme lands on the word “do.” What does that make a reader notice?</li><li>The last two lines both begin with “I am.” What does repeating those words do for the ending?</li></ol>`,key:["“Anew” and “do” rhyme. Ending on “do” puts the weight on action and on what comes next, not on the loss. (RL.7.4, impact of rhyme on a stanza)","The repeated “I am” sets “not the crop” against “what I learned.” It makes the ending sound firm, like a decision the speaker is announcing. (RL.7.4, impact of repetition)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Sale Day",std:["7.RP.A.3"],needs:["6.RP.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A $12 pumpkin is marked 25% off. What is the sale price?</li><li>A fall wreath costs $20 plus 8% sales tax. What is the total cost?</li><li>The farm stand sold 150 pumpkins last weekend and 180 this weekend. What is the percent increase?</li></ol>`,key:["$9. 25% of $12 is $3, and 12 − 3 = 9. (7.RP.A.3, markdown)","$21.60. 8% of $20 is $1.60, and 20 + 1.60 = 21.60. (7.RP.A.3, tax)","20%. The increase is 30, and 30 ÷ 150 = 0.20. (7.RP.A.3, percent increase)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Maze Field",std:["7.G.B.6"],needs:["6.G.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer with its units.",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${center(mazeFieldFig(),"A new maze field: a rectangle with a triangle on one end")}</div><ol class="qlist"><li>Find the area of the whole field.</li><li>Paths will take up 20% of the field. How many square meters will still be corn?</li><li>The farmer plants 8 corn plants on each square meter of the corn area. About how many plants is that?</li></ol>`,key:["3,000 square meters. Rectangle 60 × 40 = 2,400. Triangle 1/2 × 40 × 30 = 600. 2,400 + 600 = 3,000. (7.G.B.6, area of a composed figure)","2,400 square meters. 20% of 3,000 is 600, and 3,000 − 600 = 2,400. Or 80% of 3,000. (7.G.B.6, multistep real-world problem)","19,200 plants. 2,400 × 8 = 19,200. (7.G.B.6, multistep real-world problem)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Search Smart",std:["W.7.8"],needs:["W.6.8"],n:3,dir:"Say numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write number 3 on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${card("Source 1",SRC1_CARD)}</div><ol class="qlist"><li>You want to find out how much water a giant pumpkin needs each day. Which search would work best? Tell why. A: pumpkins. B: giant pumpkin water per day. C: why are pumpkins orange.</li><li>Add one more search word that would help you find pages written by experts.</li><li>Write a citation for Source 1 in this format: Last name, First name. “Title.” Publisher, Year.</li></ol>`,key:["B. It uses the key words of the question, so the results will be about watering giant pumpkins. A is too broad and C is off topic. (W.7.8, using search terms effectively)","A word such as extension, university, research, or study. Also fine: adding site:.edu. (W.7.8, search terms and credible sources)","Delgado, Anita. “Growing Giants: A Guide for First-Time Growers.” Marlow County Extension Office, 2025. Check the order and the punctuation. (W.7.8, standard format for citation)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.6.1","RL.6.4","RL.6.9","RI.6.1","RI.6.6","RI.6.9","RL.7.4"];const WRITE_STD=["W.6.2","W.6.3"];const MATH_STD=["6.RP.A.3","6.NS.A.1","6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","6.EE.C.9","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5","7.RP.A.3","7.G.B.6"];const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,"&amp;").replace(/</g,"&lt;").replace(/>/g,"&gt;").replace(/"/g,"&quot;");const dlist=rows=>`<dl style="margin:0;font-size:1.15rem;line-height:1.45">${rows.map(r=>`<dt style="font-weight:700">${r[0]}</dt><dd style="margin:0 0 8px 1em">${r[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=items=>`<ul style="margin:6px 0 0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.45">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const G_STORY=[["weigh-off","a contest where giant pumpkins are weighed to find the heaviest"],["estimate","a careful guess at a number"],["fairgrounds","the place where a county fair is held"],["entry form","the paper a person fills out to join a contest"]];const G_POEM=[["tended","took care of"],["anew","again, in a fresh way"],["knelt","got down on the knees"]];const G_INFO=[["stalks","the tall stems of corn plants"],["grid","a pattern of squares"],["crisscross","going in two directions that cross each other"],["GPS","a tool that uses satellites to show exactly where something is"],["tills","digs up and turns over the soil"],["seedlings","very young plants"]];const G_SRC=[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else’s words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["quote","the exact words from a source, inside quotation marks"],["extension office","a county office that shares farm research with the public"]];function glossHtml(st){const s=st.std;if(s.includes("RL.6.9"))return dlist(G_POEM.concat(G_STORY));if(s.includes("RL.7.4"))return dlist(G_POEM);if(s.includes("RI.6.1"))return dlist(G_INFO);if(s.includes("W.6.8")||s.includes("W.7.8"))return dlist(G_SRC);return dlist(G_STORY)}const FR={"RL.6.1":["I think ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The story says, “____.” This shows that ____."],"RI.6.1":["The article says, “____.” This explains why ____.","I can tell that ____ because the article says “____” and “____.”"],"RL.6.9":["Both the story and the poem are about ____.","The story shows this by ____. The poem shows it by ____."],"SL.6.4":["I think the farm stand should ____.","The first finding is ____.","The table also shows that ____.","So the better plan is ____."],"W.6.1":["I think the farm should ____.","One reason is ____.","The table shows that ____.","Another reason is ____.","That is why ____."],"W.6.2":["Raising a giant pumpkin takes ____.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","As a result, ____.","By October, ____."],"W.6.3":["The corn was so tall that ____.","“____,” said ____.","“____,” ____ answered.","A moment later, ____.","In the end, ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.find(x=>FR[x]);return`<ul class="starters">${(FR[k]||[]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function multBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){const s=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");let i=0;const num=()=>{if(s[i]==="("){i++;const v=expr();if(s[i]===")")i++;return v}if(s[i]==="-"){i++;return-num()}const m=/^(\d+\.?\d*|\.\d+)/.exec(s.slice(i));if(!m)throw new Error("n");i+=m[0].length;return+m[0]};const term=()=>{let v=num();while(s[i]==="*"||s[i]==="/"){const o=s[i++],x=num();v=o==="*"?v*x:v/x}return v};const expr=()=>{let v=term();while(s[i]==="+"||s[i]==="-"){const o=s[i++],x=term();v=o==="+"?v+x:v-x}return v};if(!s)return"";try{const v=expr();return i<s.length||!Number.isFinite(v)?"Error":String(+v.toPrecision(10))}catch(e){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","C","1","2","3","−","(","0",".","=","+",")"];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Adds, subtracts, multiplies, and divides. It has no percent, fraction, or exponent keys.</p>`}function calcBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}let rt={lab:["",""],a:["","","","",""],b:["","","","",""]};const rin=(k,i,v,w,al)=>`<input data-rt="${k}" data-i="${i}" value="${esc(v)}" aria-label="${al}" style="width:${w};max-width:100%;font:inherit;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center;padding:4px 2px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink)">`;function ratioHtml(){return`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:0 auto">${["a","b"].map((k,j)=>`<tr><th>${rin("lab",j,rt.lab[j],"6.5em","Name of row "+(j+1))}</th>${rt[k].map((v,i)=>`<td style="padding:4px">${rin(k,i,v,"3.2em","Row "+(j+1)+", box "+(i+1))}</td>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div><div style="text-align:center;margin-top:8px"><button class="btn ghost" data-rc="1">Clear the table</button></div><p class="small">Type a name for each row. Then fill in the numbers the student tells you.</p>`}function ratioBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-rt]").forEach(x=>x.oninput=()=>{rt[x.dataset.rt][+x.dataset.i]=x.value});body.querySelectorAll("[data-rc]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{rt={lab:["",""],a:["","","","",""],b:["","","","",""]};r()})}const FORM={"6.RP.A.3":["Ratios, rates, and percents",ul(["<strong>Equivalent ratios:</strong> multiply or divide both numbers by the same number.","<strong>Unit rate:</strong> the amount for 1. Divide to find it.","<strong>Percent</strong> means out of 100. 45% = 45/100 = 0.45.","part = percent × whole","Friendly percents: 10% is one tenth, 25% is one fourth, 50% is one half."])],"7.RP.A.3":["Percent problems with more than one step",ul(["Sale price = price − discount","Total = price + tax","Percent change = amount of change ÷ starting amount"])],"6.NS.C.6":["Number lines and the coordinate plane",ul(["Opposites are the same distance from 0 on different sides, like 3 and −3.","An ordered pair is (x, y). Go across first, then up or down.","Quadrant I is (+, +). Quadrant II is (−, +). Quadrant III is (−, −). Quadrant IV is (+, −).","Change the sign of x and the point flips across the y-axis. Change the sign of y and it flips across the x-axis."])],"6.EE.A.1":["Exponents and order of operations",ul(["An exponent tells how many times to use the base as a factor: 2<sup>4</sup> = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2.","Order of operations: 1. parentheses, 2. exponents, 3. multiply and divide from left to right, 4. add and subtract from left to right."])],"6.G.A.2":["Volume of a box",`<p class="formula">V = l × w × h</p><p class="formula">V = B × h, where B is the area of the base</p>`+ul(["Edge lengths can be fractions. 3 1/2 is the same as 3.5.","Volume is measured in cubic units."])],"6.EE.A.2c":["Evaluating an expression",ul(["Put the value in place of the letter, then follow the order of operations.","Exponents come before multiplying: 3x<sup>2</sup> with x = 4 is 3 × 16, not (3 × 4)<sup>2</sup>.","A formula is an expression with a name, such as A = 6s<sup>2</sup>."])],"6.G.A.1":["Area",`<p class="formula">Rectangle: A = l × w</p><p class="formula">Parallelogram: A = b × h</p><p class="formula">Triangle: A = 1/2 × b × h</p>`+ul(["The height meets the base, or the line the base sits on, at a right angle.","For other shapes, break them into rectangles and triangles and add. Or find a bigger rectangle and subtract the missing piece.","Area is measured in square units."])],"7.G.B.6":["Area",`<p class="formula">Rectangle: A = l × w</p><p class="formula">Triangle: A = 1/2 × b × h</p>`+ul(["For a shape made of parts, find each area, then add."])]};function formHtml(st){return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>`<h4 style="margin:8px 0 2px">${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`).join("")}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:READ_STD.concat(["W.6.8","W.7.8"]),html:glossHtml},frames:{name:"Sentence frames",std:Object.keys(FR),html:framesHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:multBind},form:{name:"Formula sheet",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ratio:{name:"Ratio table helper",std:["6.RP.A.3","7.RP.A.3"],html:ratioHtml,bind:ratioBind},calc:{name:"Calculator (four-function)",std:["6.RP.A.3","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5","7.RP.A.3","7.G.B.6"],html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"gloss",std:["RL.6.4"],msg:"Word help leaves out the words the Heavy Words questions ask about, so those answers are still the student’s own."},{t:"gloss",std:["W.6.8"],msg:"Word help defines credible, plagiarism, paraphrase, and quote. This stop then shows using those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",std:["RL.6.1","RI.6.1","RL.6.9"],msg:"Sentence frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["SL.6.4"]),msg:"Sentence frames supply the order, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["6.EE.A.1"],msg:"The chart gives multiplication facts. The student still has to know what the exponent means and which step comes first."},{t:"ratio",std:["6.RP.A.3"],msg:"Tables of equivalent ratios are part of this standard, so the helper does not lower the rating. Note whether the student decided what to put in it."},{t:"calc",std:["6.EE.A.1"],msg:"This stop checks computing with exponents in the right order. With the calculator, rate only whether the student chose the right steps, and note that the arithmetic was supported."},{t:"calc",std:["6.RP.A.3","6.G.A.1","7.RP.A.3","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set up each problem correctly, such as which numbers to divide or how to write the percent."},{t:"form",std:["6.NS.C.6"],msg:"The formula sheet names the four quadrants and the reflection rule, so count the quadrant and reflection answers as supported."},{t:"form",std:["6.RP.A.3","6.EE.A.1","6.G.A.1","7.RP.A.3","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The formula sheet gives the formulas and rules for this stop, so the stop shows applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"form",std:["6.G.A.2","6.EE.A.2c"],msg:"The sheet supplies the volume formula and the rule that exponents come before multiplying, which is most of what these two items check. Count them as supported, not independent."},{t:"calc",std:["6.SP.B.5"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic for the range. Rate on whether the student knows what the median and the range describe."},{t:"frames",std:["W.6.1"],msg:"The frames supply the claim opener, the reason words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:READ_STD.concat(["W.6.8"])}}});
